          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded writes into the fixed per-client write buffer corrupt the heap\n\nclient->wbuf is allocated with exactly max_request_size bytes, but SUBACK/UNSUBACK generation, retained-message replay, session resume replay and publish fan-out all append to wbuf + towrite without checking the remaining capacity. A retained PUBLISH close to max_request_size followed by a SUBSCRIBE (or two publishes delivered before the event loop flushes) writes past the allocation, corrupting heap metadata and adjacent objects.\n\nSeverity: critical\n\nCategory: Memory corruption\n\nWeaknesses: CWE-787, CWE-122\n\nRemediation:\nTrack the remaining capacity for wbuf and fail or disconnect when a reply would exceed it; grow the buffer dynamically for queued messages. Validate retained and fan-out message sizes against the destination's free space before packing.\n\nRemediation tests:\n- Publish a retained message of size max_request_size - small, then subscribe from another connection under ASan; assert no overflow and either delivery or a clean rejection.\n- Queue two near-maximum publishes for one subscriber before its socket drains; assert the write path bounds the buffer.\n- Add a unit test that asserts every append checks towrite + len <= max_request_size.\n\nPreventive controls:\n- Centralize all writes to wbuf in a helper that returns an error when the destination is full.\n- Size reply buffers from the actual packet being built and enforce a per-client output quota."
          },
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "codexSecurity/v1": "codex-security/v1:sha256:2e3a37c8148ecddd1b11c6309400b56810b018250c1ddab25b628ae33e0b45b5"
